The query optimiser decides which index to use based on the query shape and the available indices. If there are multiple candidate plans for a given query shape, a trial evaluation will run to determine which index returns the initial batch of results (101 documents) with the least amount of "work" measured. The winning plan will be cached and periodically reevaluated.

Plan evaluation only considers which candidate plan returns the initial batch of results (101 documents) with less overall effort. The query planner does not run the plans to completion during evaluation, or keep any metrics on the distribution of key values. The cached query plan would have been based on a comparison where the second index did not not have to perform the collection scan.

Deleting a pod also removes the query planner cache, forcing a re-evaluation. Since neither index is ideal for the given query, Mongo might mistakenly come to the conclusion that the wrong of the two indices is more suitable for the given query because it happens to perform better on the first batch of results.